WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.100
ED GLAESER: At the end of the 19th century,

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.750
the Russian empire was vast, poor, and largely agricultural.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.230
Per capita income was about 1,500 modern dollars,

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.930
and the average peasant made do with much less than that,

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.250
suggesting poverty comparable to the more desperate parts of Africa today.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:22.280
The average Englishman earned 4.6 times more than the average Russian in 1890.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.720
Romanoff Russia was overwhelmingly rural.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.530
Only 13.4% of the empire was urban as late as 1897.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:33.840
This nation of 125 million souls had only two cities with more than 200,000

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.310
inhabitants in 1890.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.120
St. Petersburg, with one million, and Moscow with 800,000.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.880
And these were largely imperial cities, although, Moscow

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.240
had become the center of Russia&amp;#39;s nascent textile industry.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.310
Gradually, this backward giant was beginning

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.560
to industrialize and urbanize.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.410
Russian weakness during the Crimean War taught the Czar

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.320
that rural societies have difficulty competing militarily

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.230
with industrial powerhouses, like England and France.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.500
In 1861, Alexander the Second liberated the serfs.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.640
This meant that peasants were, in principle, free to move to cities.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.440
Although in practice, their former masters

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.610
still demanded significant payment in exchange for allowing their escape.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:15.570
In the 1880s and 1890s, Sergei Witte tried to thrust Russia

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.040
into the industrial age.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.990
Under Witte and Tsar Nicholas the Second,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.980
Russia built railways even across the Siberian vastness.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.960
Tariffs protected infant industries.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.370
Foreign financing flowed into the empire and cities started to grow.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.270
Kiev and Odessa both reached a half a million inhabitants in 1910.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.520
These cities were built up by local magnets,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.380
like the Brodskys, who were sugar barons like New York City&amp;#39;s Havemeyers.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.650
Ekaterinoslav, now Dnipropetrovsk, became a Russian Pittsburgh,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.230
combining coal brought by rail from Donetsk,

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:49.650
and iron mined at nearby Krivoy Rog.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.260
Ekaterinoslav, whose name honors Catherine the Great,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.360
proved an early model for the Russian industrial city.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.430
A formerly remote place with access to natural resources that became

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.610
connected to the wider world by railroads.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.040
As the early anti modernization czars like,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.000
Nicholas the First were well aware, cities pose risks to autocrats.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.960
And revolutions broke out in Odessa and St. Petersburg in 1905.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.380
[? Industriolwov ?] in Romanov controlled Poland rebelled as well.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.590
Some farmers also rose up, but a general strike in the capital

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.260
is far more threatening that any rural contretemps.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.380
The Romanovs survived that round of uprisings

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.530
through a combination of appeasement and repression.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.220
But Nicholas the Second lost his throne and his life when St. Petersburg

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.030
rebelled not once, but twice, in 1917.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.820
World War I had left the czars without enough loyal troops

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.100
to dominate the organized urban workers.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.310
The revolution succeeded, just like most successful revolutions,

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.370
because the troops had lost their loyalty to the Czar,

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.700
and were unwilling to fire on the unhappy urbanites.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.510
The chaos of the Russian Revolution shriveled Russia&amp;#39;s great cities

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:59.280
and shrank per capita incomes to blow 1,000 modern dollars by 1921.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.690
The population of St. Petersburg shrank from over 2 million to under 800,000.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.100
Moscow&amp;#39;s population dropped from 1.8 million to just over 1 million.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.330
The new Bolshevik masters of Russia had to rebuild the economy.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:15.690
Lenin&amp;#39;s new economic policy was a profoundly mixed model.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.230
More freedom for farmers to sell their surplus grain, plus state

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:25.380
owned heavy industry and a small entrepreneurial urban class of NEPmen.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.050
Russia&amp;#39;s incomes recovered to pre-war levels.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.300
And the cities came back as well.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.000
Since Moscow was now the capital, its imperial status

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.260
turned it into Russia&amp;#39;s megacity, with a population that exceeded 4 million

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.790
by 1939.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.730
When Lenin died and Stalin pushed his way to power,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.280
this new red czar wanted his nation&amp;#39;s industrial might

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.200
to rival the Western powers.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.070
Starting in 1928, he instituted a series of five year plans

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.680
that would drive Russian manufacturing into the 20th century.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.060
These five year plans made huge factories and cities that

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.150
were built to enable mass production.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.220
Stalin didn&amp;#39;t mind starving millions.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.710
And he certainly didn&amp;#39;t care about urban quality of life,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.480
or perhaps, life more generally.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.730
Many of the industrial cities were in the east to take

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.940
advantage of mineral deposits and coursing waterways.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.520
During World War II, even more production facilities

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.959
were moved toward Siberia to avoid the threat of Hitler&amp;#39;s Luftwaffe.

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:21.450
In some cases, Stalin&amp;#39;s industrialization

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.270
expanded existing population centers.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.740
Yekaterinburg had been founded in 1723 on the border of Europe and Asia.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.100
Its name honored the wife of Peter the Great.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.440
And it was where the last czar and his family were executed.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.330
In 1933, it became the home of Ural Mash, the Euro heavy machine building

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:43.350
plant, which made blast furnaces, rolling mills, and cranes for mining

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.120
cities even further to the east.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.010
It also built military materiel, including the famously durable T-34

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.630
tanks.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.760
Further east, cities like Novosibirsk, the Chicago of Siberia, and Krasnoyarsk

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.520
expanded enormously during the 1930s.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.290
Novosibirsk had only been founded in 1893,

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.980
but it was a natural transportation hub since it sat next to the bridge

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:07.870
over the mighty river Ob.

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:11.080
Krasnoyarsk is about 450 miles further to the east.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.150
And it became a center of the gulag system,

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.630
where millions were forced to work in Stalin&amp;#39;s factories and mines.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.240
In 1956, Krasnoyarsk&amp;#39;s industrial advantage

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.960
was increased by the opening of an awesome hydroelectric dam.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.080
Stalin&amp;#39;s industrialisation created a very special type

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.420
of city, the gulag city.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.430
Population clusters around factories that

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.920
were developed by and for penal labor.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.240
Norilsk may be the most extreme of Stalin&amp;#39;s industrial towns.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.190
It is the world&amp;#39;s northernmost city with over 100,000 inhabitants,

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:45.880
and it did not even exist before World War I. Norilsk&amp;#39;s great advantage lay

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.630
underground in vast Siberian mines that contain enormous amounts of nickel,

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.750
copper, cobalt, and palladium.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.260
A railway was built to connect the city with a nearby port of Dudinka.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:59.290
And from there, the minerals could be shipped by sea to Murmansk,

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.100
or up the river Yenisei to Krasnoyarsk.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.380
Gulag labor worked Norilsk&amp;#39;s mines under often horrific conditions.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.970
More than 15,000 workers died from cold and starvation.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:16.090
In 1953 after Stalin&amp;#39;s death, Norilsk rose up in nonviolent protest.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:18.190
Not much came of that.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:22.360
The gulag system became slightly more humane under Khrushchev.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.440
But these eastern industrial cities continued producing metal and machines

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:29.470
and misery throughout the Soviet era.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:33.340
In the United States, rust belt cities like Cleveland and Detroit

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:37.270
suffer because their industries have shrunk or relocated.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:39.580
The very success of those industries had crowded

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.467
out other local entrepreneurship.

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.300
And their locations are far from the sunnier

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.880
climates that most Americans prefer.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.440
The problems facing Norilsk and Krasnoyarsk would seem to be far worse.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.690
There never was any entrepreneurship in Norilsk.

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:53.770
And the climate is awful.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:55.570
The air is terribly polluted.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:59.800
After 1991, Russian mobility increased, and people and firms

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:01.000
were largely free to move.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.700
Moscow alone restricted immigration, at least

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.730
nominally this migration offers the possibility

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.120
of eventually correcting any extreme geographic imbalances created

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:12.040
by Soviet industrialization.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.240
Yet, Norilsk&amp;#39;s population remains at 170,000.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.050
Workers stay because the mines offer high wages, wages that are apparently

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.240
worth losing years of life for.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.090
Novosibirsk and Krasnoyarsk continue to grow.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.150
Russia remains highly dependent on natural resources.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.670
And many of those resources are in Siberia.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.180
It is hard not to think that eventually these cities will

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:37.660
see the same shrinkage experienced in the American rust belt.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:40.180
If Russia becomes richer, and its economy shifts away

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.970
from natural resources, then Russians will presumably also

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:46.660
move to warmer, more humane, consumer cities.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:51.220
Yet for now, Stalin&amp;#39;s Siberian industrial cities remain.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.030
Monuments to a Russia that cared more for industrial success

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.810
than for human life and happiness.

